Hi,

I'm currently digging into some odd latencies experienced with the CFS scheduler
on a 2.6.33.2 kernel. First, it is probably nothing new specific to 2.6.33, it
might very well affect a larger set of kernel versions too. I'll describe the
problem and possible solutions here, and will provide the code and patches if
people think the general approach makes sense.

I'm using a modified version of a "wakeup latency" program that spawns a
short-lived thread every 10ms with timer_create() and detects latencies longer
than 5ms. It also detects timer overruns. As I configured it, the workload it
performs when it wakes up is close to nothing.

First, I tried this program in parallel with a make -j10 workload on a SMP 8-way
machine I access remotely. It ran more or less fine (no large latencies
detected). Then, I tried running it on my laptop (preparing my ELC LTTng
tutorial presentation). It's a uniprocessor machine. I started experiencing
relatively long latencies (in the order of 60ms) when I was doing some
Xorg-related tasks concurrently: resizing windows and/or starting firefox.

maximum latency: 56111.4 탎
average latency: 2752.9 탎
missed timer events: 0

Important configuration bits (will provide the full config at the end of the
email):

CONFIG_NO_HZ=y
CONFIG_HIGH_RES_TIMERS=y
CONFIG_HZ_300=y
CONFIG_PREEMPT=y

So I decided to gather tracing information with LTTng to pinpoint what was going
wrong in the time window right before these longer latencies. After a few
tracing iterations, by eventually adding vruntime instrumentation to CFS, I
figured out that the root cause of the problem seems to be that Xorg, being a
long-lived process that is usually doing nothing, has a vruntime value way, way
below the vruntime value of the newly spawned wakeup-latency threads (at least a
few minutes below).  However, when it is activated by window changes or firefox
start, it grabs a lot of CPU time all at once and causes these long latencies.

I first thought that the goal of the "ideal_runtime" check in
check_preempt_tick() was to ensure that no such thread could eat too much CPU
time at once. However, looking at the trace execution pattern in the interesting
area tells me that Xorg ran _most_ of the time, but has been scheduled out
(waiting for I/O) a few times during the interesting time period. Each time Xorg
is scheduled out for a short amount of time, and then back in, the
prev_sum_exec_runtime is reset, and therefore this threshold is ineffective.

So, I tried to come up with a solution that would kill these latencies. I found
the following way to ensure that threads will not lag behind in terms of
vruntime values for much longer than the sched_latency_ns period (5ms). For now,
the solution I have found is to "scale" the vruntime value returned by
calc_delta_fair so the amount added is not nanoseconds, but rather an amount
scaled relatively to the difference between max_vruntime and min_vruntime:

(added max_vruntime calculation is implicit here)

static void update_min_vruntime(struct cfs_rq *cfs_rq)
{
....
        cfs_rq->scale_vruntime = max_t(u64, 1,
                                       div_u64(cfs_rq->max_vruntime
                                               - cfs_rq->min_vruntime,
                                               sysctl_sched_latency));
}

static inline unsigned long
calc_delta_fair(unsigned long delta, struct sched_entity *se)
{
        struct cfs_rq *cfs_rq = cfs_rq_of(se);

        if (unlikely(se->load.weight != NICE_0_LOAD))
                delta = calc_delta_mine(delta, NICE_0_LOAD, &se->load);
        delta *= cfs_rq->scale_vruntime;
        return delta;
}

The result I get with this patch applied is:

maximum latency: 21889.6 탎
average latency: 1083.1 탎
missed timer events: 1

The maximum latency has clearly diminished (I am unable to reach the kind of
very long latencies I was able to get previously), but there seems to be
slightly more timer overrun events, for a reason I have not yet identified.

So before I dig further in that direction, I'd like to know if my approach to
try solving the problem by scaling the vruntime delta makes sense at all. By the
way, I am well aware of the deadline scheduler work, and I think it might help
at some point, but for now I am mostly interested in getting, if possible, the
CFS scheduler to have lower latencies on common desktop environment (e.g.
running Xorg and playing music).

Thanks,

Mathieu

